{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The whole change is in `build_system()` at `bot.py:130`.\n\n**Root cause:** the static `INSTRUCTIONS` (~990 tokens, never changes) and the dynamic athlete context (including long-term facts) were concatenated into a single system-prompt block with one `cache_control` marker. Since prompt caching matches the request prefix up to each marker as one segment, any `save_fact` — even a 15-token fact — changed that block's text, invalidated the whole segment, and forced the next call to pay cache-write price for the entire system prompt. That's the `cache_read_input_tokens: 0` / `cache_creation_input_tokens ~1000+` pattern on lines 5, 10, 14, 16, 23, 29 of `logs/usage.jsonl`.\n\n**Fix:** the system prompt is now two blocks with a cache breakpoint between them:\n- Block 1: the static `INSTRUCTIONS` — cached once, stays valid forever\n- Block 2: the athlete context (date, profile, zones, goal, facts) — marked too, so unchanged contexts are also served from cache\n\nA memory write now only re-creates the small context block; the instructions segment remains a cache hit, cutting the cost of a `save_fact` from ~1.25× the full prompt to ~1.25× the facts delta. Tool selection and the message loop are untouched (keyword tool filtering stays, per NOTES.md).\n\n**Verification** (no external calls, per your ground rules): asserted block 1 is byte-identical for athletes with different fact lists; ran `reply()` end-to-end with a stub LLM emulating Anthropic prefix caching — a `save_fact` round trip completes with the instructions segment still a cache hit; `import bot` passes with the SDK absent. Test artifacts were reverted, so only `bot.py` is modified, plus the requested `ANSWER.md` with the full write-up."}
